const FRAMEWORKS = [
  {
    "id": 1,
    "slug": "fw-01",
    "title": "RISE: Research \u2192 Insight \u2192 Strategy \u2192 Execution",
    "subtitle": "A disciplined planning cycle from data to action",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "RISE structures marketing (and strategy) planning as four sequenced stages. Research: gather evidence \u2014 market data, customer interviews, competitive intelligence, performance analytics \u2014 before forming views. Insight: distil the research into a small number of non-obvious truths about customers, category or competition; an insight is a 'why' that reframes the problem, not a restated statistic. Strategy: make choices built on the insight \u2014 target, positioning, resource allocation, the few battles to fight. Execution: translate strategy into campaigns, content, channels and metrics, with feedback loops back into research. The model's discipline is the gate between stages: no strategy without a genuine insight, no execution without explicit strategic choices \u2014 a corrective to organisations that leap from data to campaigns.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a four-step forward arrow \u2014 Research \u2192 Insight \u2192 Strategy \u2192 Execution \u2014 with a feedback arrow returning from Execution to Research to close the loop, and a gate icon between each pair of stages listing the question that must be answered to pass ('So what?' after Research; 'Which choices?' after Insight; 'On brief?' after Strategy). Teach the gates, not the boxes.",
    "examples": "1. Dove 'Real Beauty'. Research: global study on women and beauty. Insight: only ~2% of women called themselves beautiful \u2014 the category sold anxiety. Strategy: position on real beauty. Execution: two decades of coherent campaigns.\n2. Cadbury Dairy Milk 'Kuch Meetha Ho Jaaye'. Research into Indian sweet-eating occasions produced the insight that chocolate competed with traditional mithai at celebrations, not with candy \u2014 strategy repositioned chocolate for adult festive moments, execution followed for years.\n3. Swiggy's late-night positioning. Order-time data (research) revealed a distinct midnight-craving occasion (insight); dedicated late-night creative and assortment (strategy/execution) followed.\n4. Old Spice's turnaround. Research showed women bought most men's body wash; the insight \u2014 speak to her, entertain him \u2014 produced 'The Man Your Man Could Smell Like'.\n5. A failed launch post-mortem. Autopsy by stage: was the research thin, the insight a restated stat, the strategy choice- less, or the execution off-strategy? The model localises the failure.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Building the annual marketing or brand plan from evidence rather than habit\n\u2022 Turning around a brand whose campaigns are activity-rich but results-poor\n\u2022 Structuring a consulting or agency engagement so the client sees a clear line from data to recommendation\n\u2022 Running a campaign post-mortem to locate whether the failure was in research, insight, strategy or execution\n\u2022 Onboarding a new leadership team that needs a shared, disciplined planning language",
    "when_not": "\u2022 When the decision is urgent and reversible \u2014 a full research cycle costs more than acting and correcting; run a fast test instead\n\u2022 When rich, recent research already exists \u2014 repeating the R stage is procrastination dressed as rigour\n\u2022 In genuinely novel categories where customers cannot articulate needs \u2014 research describes the present, not the discontinuous future\n\u2022 As a substitute for judgement: the model sequences thinking but cannot generate the insight itself",
    "sources": "\u2022 Smith, P. R. (1998). Marketing Communications: An Integrated Approach. Kogan Page \u2014 origin of the SOSTAC\u00ae planning system (Situation, Objectives, Strategy, Tactics, Action, Control), the documented planning cycle from which practitioner Research\u2013 Insight\u2013Strategy\u2013Execution models derive.\n\u2022 Kotler, P. & Keller, K. L. (2016). Marketing Management (15th ed.). Pearson \u2014 the classic marketing-planning cycle (analysis \u2192 planning \u2192 implementation \u2192 control) that underpins RISE.\n\u2022 Note: RISE (Research \u2192 Insight \u2192 Strategy \u2192 Execution) is a practitioner planning discipline with no single canonical academic origin; it is a restatement of the established planning cycle above."
  },
  {
    "id": 2,
    "slug": "fw-02",
    "title": "PESTEL Analysis",
    "subtitle": "Scanning the macro-environment",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "PESTEL structures the scan of the macro-environment into six categories: Political (government stability, trade policy), Economic (growth, inflation, exchange and interest rates), Social (demographics, values, lifestyles), Technological (innovation, automation, R&D trajectories), Environmental (climate, sustainability pressures) and Legal (regulation, competition and labour law). These are forces no single firm controls but every firm must anticipate. The output is not six lists but a small set of prioritised drivers of change \u2014 trends whose direction and impact materially affect the industry \u2014 which then feed scenario planning, Five Forces (structure may shift), and the opportunity/threat half of SWOT.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic shows the organisation at the centre ringed by six labelled segments \u2014 Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Environmental, Legal. Use the ring to make two points: the forces act on the firm from outside its control, and they interact with each other. In application, have students annotate each segment with concrete, industry- relevant trends and then star the few that qualify as key drivers of change.",
    "examples": "1. EV transition. Automakers face simultaneous PESTEL shifts: subsidies and emission mandates (P/L), battery cost curves (T), climate expectations (E/S) and fuel-price economics (Ec) \u2014 collectively restructuring the industry.\n2. UPI in India. A political-technological driver \u2014 public digital infrastructure \u2014 created the fintech boom; firms that read the PESTEL signal early (PhonePe, Paytm) captured the opportunity.\n3. Tobacco and ESG. Decades of social and legal pressure (S/L) pushed tobacco firms into next-generation products \u2014 a PESTEL-driven strategic pivot rather than a market-driven one.\n4. Semiconductor geopolitics. Export controls and CHIPS-style subsidies (P) now shape fab location decisions more than cost \u2014 a vivid case of politics overriding economics.\n5. GDPR and data businesses. A legal driver that restructured ad-tech economics globally, advantaging players with first- party data.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Assessing whether to enter a new country or geographic market\n\u2022 Building 3\u20135 year scenarios for a long-horizon capital investment (plant, network, infrastructure)\n\u2022 Stress-testing strategy in regulated sectors \u2014 pharma, banking, energy, telecom \u2014 where policy shifts change economics\n\u2022 Anticipating how sustainability, demographic or technology shifts will reshape demand in the core business\n\u2022 Setting the external-context assumptions that underpin a board-level strategic plan 7",
    "when_not": "\u2022 For short-horizon operational decisions \u2014 macro trends move too slowly to inform quarterly choices\n\u2022 As a competitor or industry analysis \u2014 PESTEL scans the macro layer; use Five Forces for industry structure\n\u2022 When it degenerates into an unprioritised list of trends with no link to the specific business decision\n\u2022 In isolation for entry decisions \u2014 it identifies conditions, not whether the firm can win there",
    "sources": "\u2022 Aguilar, F. J. (1967). Scanning the Business Environment. Macmillan \u2014 introduced the original 'ETPS' taxonomy (Economic, Technical, Political, Social) that evolved into PEST and later PESTEL.\n\u2022 Johnson, G., Whittington, R. & Scholes, K. (2017). Exploring Strategy (11th ed.). Pearson \u2014 standard treatment of PESTEL and the identification of key drivers of change."
  },
  {
    "id": 3,
    "slug": "fw-03",
    "title": "SWOT Analysis",
    "subtitle": "Matching internal capabilities to external conditions",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "SWOT organises a strategic situation into four cells: Strengths and Weaknesses, which are internal to the organisation, and Opportunities and Threats, which arise from the external environment. Its power lies not in listing items but in the matching step: using strengths to capture opportunities (SO strategies), fixing or hedging weaknesses that block opportunities (WO), deploying strengths to blunt threats (ST), and defending where weaknesses meet threats (WT). Because it is simple and universally understood, SWOT is often the integrating summary at the end of deeper analyses \u2014 Five Forces and PESTEL feed the external half; value chain and VRIO feed the internal half.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a 2\u00d72 grid: columns split Helpful vs Harmful factors, rows split Internal vs External origin, yielding Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats. When teaching, extend it into the TOWS matching matrix \u2014 SO, WO, ST, WT strategy cells \u2014 to show students that the grid is the input, and the strategies generated from pairings are the output.",
    "examples": "1. Tata Motors & JLR. Strength in low-cost engineering plus the opportunity of premium-brand acquisition (JLR) produced an SO strategy; the threat of EV disruption meeting a weakness in software talent defines its current WT agenda.\n2. Netflix circa 2011. Strength in streaming technology matched the broadband opportunity, justifying the painful pivot away from the DVD business despite short-term shareholder backlash.\n3. Zomato. Strength in delivery network density met the quick-commerce opportunity, producing Blinkit's acquisition (SO); dependence on discount-driven demand remains a weakness against the threat of well-funded rivals.\n4. Kodak (cautionary). Kodak's own labs identified digital imaging as both opportunity and threat; the failure was not analysis but the unwillingness to act on a WT position in film.\n5. A regional B-school. Useful non-corporate illustration: strong industry ties (S), rising demand for analytics education (O), limited research faculty (W), and global online programmes (T).",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Kicking off an annual strategic-planning cycle with a shared situation assessment\n\u2022 Evaluating a specific decision \u2014 a launch, an acquisition, a market exit \u2014 before committing resources\n\u2022 Integrating the outputs of deeper analyses (Five Forces, PESTEL, VRIO) into one decision-ready summary\n\u2022 Aligning a cross-functional leadership team on priorities during a turnaround\n\u2022 Framing strategy for a non-profit or public body where profit-based tools fit poorly",
    "when_not": "\u2022 As the primary analysis \u2014 SWOT is a synthesis of deeper work, not a source of insight on its own\n\u2022 When items cannot be evidenced or benchmarked against competitors ('good quality' is opinion, not analysis)\n\u2022 For prioritisation decisions \u2014 the grid ranks nothing; ten unweighted strengths give no guidance\n\u2022 When internal politics will censor the W and T cells \u2014 an unhonest SWOT is worse than none",
    "sources": "\u2022 Learned, E. P., Christensen, C. R., Andrews, K. R. & Guth, W. D. (1969). Business Policy: Text and Cases. Richard D. Irwin \u2014 the Harvard 'LCAG' framework matching organisational capability to environment, the intellectual basis of SWOT.\n\u2022 Humphrey, A. S. (2005). 'SWOT Analysis for Management Consulting.' SRI Alumni Newsletter, SRI International \u2014 Humphrey's recollection of the SOFT/SWOT work at the Stanford Research Institute in the 1960s.\n\u2022 Note: the precise origin of the SWOT acronym is historically debated; the sources above are the most commonly cited."
  },
  {
    "id": 4,
    "slug": "fw-04",
    "title": "Porter's Five Forces",
    "subtitle": "Assessing industry attractiveness and competitive pressure",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "Michael Porter's Five Forces framework (1979) explains why some industries are structurally more profitable than others. Industry profitability is shaped by five competitive forces: the intensity of rivalry among existing competitors, the threat of new entrants, the threat of substitute products or services, the bargaining power of buyers, and the bargaining power of suppliers. The stronger the forces, the more industry profits are competed away or captured by others in the value system. Analysing each force \u2014 and the structural drivers behind it, such as entry barriers, switching costs, product differentiation and concentration \u2014 lets a firm judge industry attractiveness, anticipate shifts in profitability, and position itself where the forces are weakest or reshape them in its favour.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic places Competitive Rivalry at the centre, with four arrows pressing inward: Threat of New Entrants (top), Bargaining Power of Suppliers (left), Bargaining Power of Buyers (right), and Threat of Substitutes (bottom). Teach students to annotate each box with the 2\u20133 structural drivers that make that force strong or weak in the industry being analysed, then rate each force high/medium/low to reach an overall attractiveness verdict.",
    "examples": "1. Airlines. Fierce rivalry, powerful suppliers (Boeing/Airbus, labour, airports), price-sensitive buyers, and substitutes such as rail keep industry returns chronically low despite huge revenues.\n2. Indian telecom after Jio. Reliance Jio's 2016 entry showed how one new entrant with deep pockets can intensify rivalry, collapse prices and force industry consolidation from a dozen players to three.\n3. Soft drinks concentrate makers. Coca-Cola and PepsiCo occupy a structurally attractive position: bottlers and retailers face powerful, differentiated suppliers, while entry into brand-driven concentrate is extremely hard.\n4. Smartphone assembly vs. design. Contract assemblers (Foxconn) face brutal buyer power from Apple; Apple itself faces weak supplier power and high buyer switching costs \u2014 same value chain, opposite force profiles.\n5. Quick-commerce in India. Blinkit, Zepto and Instamart illustrate low entry barriers, intense rivalry and powerful gig- labour and real-estate suppliers compressing margins.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Deciding whether to enter, invest in, or exit an industry\n\u2022 Conducting commercial due diligence on an acquisition or investment target\n\u2022 Explaining to the board why margins in a business are structurally high or low\n\u2022 Anticipating how deregulation, a new entrant or a technology shift will change industry profitability\n\u2022 Designing moves that reshape the forces in the firm's favour \u2014 raising switching costs, tying up distribution 11",
    "when_not": "\u2022 For analysing a single company's performance \u2014 the unit of analysis is the industry, not the firm\n\u2022 In fast-moving platform or ecosystem markets where industry boundaries blur and redraw within quarters\n\u2022 For very short-term tactical pricing or campaign decisions \u2014 structure explains long-run profitability\n\u2022 When the 'industry' cannot be sensibly defined \u2014 a mis-scoped market makes every force rating wrong",
    "sources": "\u2022 Porter, M. E. (1979). 'How Competitive Forces Shape Strategy.' Harvard Business Review, 57(2), 137\u2013145.\n\u2022 Porter, M. E. (1980). Competitive Strategy: Techniques for Analyzing Industries and Competitors. Free Press."
  },
  {
    "id": 5,
    "slug": "fw-05",
    "title": "Value Chain Analysis (Porter)",
    "subtitle": "Locating where value is created and cost is incurred",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "Porter's value chain disaggregates a firm into strategically relevant activities: five primary activities (inbound logistics, operations, outbound logistics, marketing & sales, service) that create and deliver the product, and four support activities (firm infrastructure, human resource management, technology development, procurement) that enable them. Margin is what remains when the collective cost of performing activities is subtracted from the value buyers pay. Competitive advantage lives at the activity level: a firm outperforms rivals either by performing activities at lower cost or by performing them in ways that create superior differentiation. Linkages between activities \u2014 and with suppliers' and channels' chains \u2014 are often the hardest advantages to imitate.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is the classic arrow: the bottom band shows the five primary activities flowing left to right toward Margin at the arrowhead; the upper bands show the four support activities stretching across all primary activities to signal that they enable the whole chain. In teaching, have students write each activity's cost share and its contribution to buyer value \u2014 the asymmetries revealed are where strategy discussions start.",
    "examples": "1. Zara. Advantage comes from linkages: co-located design and production, small batches and rapid logistics make the whole chain fast \u2014 no single activity explains it.\n2. Amul. The cooperative's chain from village milk collection through cold logistics to national brands shows value-chain design as strategy, sharing margin back to farmers.\n3. IKEA. Reconfigures the chain by transferring assembly and delivery to customers, spending the savings on design and scale \u2014 a textbook cost-advantage architecture.\n4. Apple. Keeps design, chip architecture and retail (high differentiation value) while outsourcing manufacturing \u2014 choosing which activities to own by their contribution to willingness-to-pay.\n5. McDonald's vs fine dining. Same primary activities, opposite drivers: one chain engineered for consistency and cost, the other for experience \u2014 useful for showing activity systems must fit one strategy.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Diagnosing whether a firm's advantage truly comes from cost or from differentiation\n\u2022 Deciding what to outsource, offshore or vertically integrate\n\u2022 Benchmarking activity-level costs against competitors to find the gap\n\u2022 Identifying where digital technology or AI could reconfigure how activities are performed\n\u2022 Mapping sustainability or ethics exposure (emissions, labour) along the chain",
    "when_not": "13\n\u2022 For startups with little activity structure yet \u2014 there is no chain to disaggregate before product\u2013market fit\n\u2022 When cost data cannot be allocated to activities \u2014 without numbers the chain becomes a decorative org chart\n\u2022 As a substitute for customer analysis \u2014 the chain shows where value is made, not what buyers value\n\u2022 In pure platform businesses where value is created by network participants, not a linear activity sequence \u2014 adapt or use a different lens",
    "sources": "\u2022 Porter, M. E. (1985). Competitive Advantage: Creating and Sustaining Superior Performance. Free Press \u2014 introduces the value chain and the value system."
  },
  {
    "id": 6,
    "slug": "fw-06",
    "title": "Porter's Generic Strategies",
    "subtitle": "Choosing how to compete: cost, differentiation, or focus",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "Porter argues that superior performance requires a clear choice among three generic strategies. Cost leadership: become the lowest-cost producer in the industry and win through price or margin. Differentiation: be unique on dimensions buyers value widely, earning a price premium that exceeds the cost of being unique. Focus: target a narrow segment and serve it better than broad-scope rivals, via either cost focus or differentiation focus. The framework's sharpest claim is the danger of being 'stuck in the middle' \u2014 firms that pursue both advantages half- heartedly typically achieve neither, because the organisational requirements (culture, systems, investments) of cost and differentiation conflict.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a 2\u00d72: horizontal axis = source of advantage (lower cost vs differentiation), vertical axis = competitive scope (broad vs narrow). Broad row yields Cost Leadership and Differentiation; narrow row yields Cost Focus and Differentiation Focus. Draw 'stuck in the middle' as the void at the centre. Ask students to place 8\u201310 named firms on the grid and defend the placements \u2014 misplacements reveal the concept gaps.",
    "examples": "1. IndiGo vs Vistara. IndiGo's single-fleet, high-utilisation cost leadership consistently outperformed full-service differentiators in Indian aviation \u2014 and absorbed them.\n2. Rolex. Differentiation focus: a narrow luxury segment, uniqueness built on craft and scarcity, and pricing power that cost players cannot touch.\n3. Aldi/Lidl vs Tesco. Hard-discount cost leadership with narrow assortment forced full-range incumbents to cut prices and imitate private label \u2014 cost leaders reset industry price expectations.\n4. Shouldice Hospital. Focus classic: one procedure (hernia), extraordinary outcomes and costs \u2014 and a demonstration of why broadening the scope would destroy the model.\n5. Xiaomi vs Apple. Same industry, opposite generic strategies profitably coexisting \u2014 evidence that the choice is about internal consistency, not one right answer.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Forcing an explicit competitive-positioning choice when launching or repositioning a business unit\n\u2022 Diagnosing underperformance suspected to stem from a 'stuck-in-the-middle' straddle\n\u2022 Aligning operations, HR and marketing policies behind one coherent advantage\n\u2022 Judging whether the firm could survive and win a price war\n\u2022 Assessing whether a focused niche player can defend itself as its segment grows",
    "when_not": "15\n\u2022 When the business genuinely competes on a hybrid model with proven economics \u2014 forcing a label obscures reality\n\u2022 For corporate-level (multi-business) strategy \u2014 the framework addresses how one business unit competes\n\u2022 In winner-take-all digital markets where scale and network effects, not cost-vs-differentiation choice, decide outcomes\n\u2022 As a static, one-time choice \u2014 positions must be revisited as technology shifts the cost of differentiation",
    "sources": "\u2022 Porter, M. E. (1980). Competitive Strategy: Techniques for Analyzing Industries and Competitors. Free Press \u2014 cost leadership, differentiation and focus.\n\u2022 Porter, M. E. (1985). Competitive Advantage. Free Press \u2014 elaborates the generic strategies and the 'stuck in the middle' argument."
  },
  {
    "id": 7,
    "slug": "fw-07",
    "title": "VRIO / Resource-Based View",
    "subtitle": "Testing which resources yield sustained advantage",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "The resource-based view locates competitive advantage inside the firm: in resources (assets, brands, patents, data) and capabilities (things the firm does well, like rapid product development). VRIO is Jay Barney's test of whether any of them can sustain advantage. Is the resource Valuable \u2014 does it exploit an opportunity or neutralise a threat? Is it Rare among competitors? Is it costly to Imitate \u2014 protected by history, causal ambiguity or social complexity? And is the firm Organised to capture the value? The questions are sequential: failing V means disadvantage; V alone means parity; V+R means temporary advantage; V+R+I means sustained advantage \u2014 but only if O is in place.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a stepped decision flow: four gates labelled Valuable? \u2192 Rare? \u2192 Costly to Imitate? \u2192 Organised?, each 'No' exiting to its competitive consequence (disadvantage, parity, temporary advantage, unused potential) and the final 'Yes' landing on Sustained Competitive Advantage. Walk one real resource through all four gates aloud \u2014 the discipline of the sequence is the pedagogic payload.",
    "examples": "1. Toyota Production System. Openly documented for decades yet still hard to copy \u2014 the advantage is socially complex routines and culture, a classic inimitability story.\n2. HDFC Bank's underwriting culture. Rivals could match products and technology, but its credit discipline through cycles proved rare, causally ambiguous and organisationally embedded.\n3. Xerox PARC (O failure). World-changing inventions, no capture \u2014 the canonical case that V, R and I are worthless without organisation.\n4. Ferrari's brand. Valuable, rare, protected by 70 years of racing history no competitor can re-run \u2014 path dependence as an imitation barrier.\n5. Amazon's logistics data. Data plus algorithms plus physical network form a resource bundle; any element alone is imitable, the combination is not \u2014 teaches complementarity.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Auditing which of a firm's assets and capabilities actually drive advantage\n\u2022 Testing whether a current performance edge is durable or about to be competed away\n\u2022 Framing the acquisition logic for an M&A deal \u2014 what resource is being bought and can rivals copy it?\n\u2022 Deciding which capabilities to build in-house versus buy, rent or partner for\n\u2022 Prioritising where to invest to protect an advantage before rivals catch up",
    "when_not": "17\n\u2022 Before the firm has any track record \u2014 a startup's resources are hypotheses, not testable advantages\n\u2022 For explaining short-term performance swings \u2014 VRIO addresses sustained advantage, not quarterly results\n\u2022 When applied to generic assets (cash, standard technology, 'talent') that fail the Rare test by definition\n\u2022 As a defence of the status quo \u2014 resources that pass VRIO today can be tomorrow's core rigidities",
    "sources": "\u2022 Wernerfelt, B. (1984). 'A Resource-Based View of the Firm.' Strategic Management Journal, 5(2), 171\u2013180.\n\u2022 Barney, J. B. (1991). 'Firm Resources and Sustained Competitive Advantage.' Journal of Management, 17(1), 99\u2013120.\n\u2022 Barney, J. B. (1995). 'Looking Inside for Competitive Advantage.' Academy of Management Executive, 9(4), 49\u201361 \u2014 the VRIO/VRIN articulation."
  },
  {
    "id": 8,
    "slug": "fw-08",
    "title": "Core Competence (Prahalad & Hamel)",
    "subtitle": "Building strategy around distinctive capabilities",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "Prahalad and Hamel (1990) argued that firms should be understood not as portfolios of businesses but as portfolios of competencies \u2014 deep, collective learning in coordinating skills and integrating technologies. A core competence must pass three tests: it provides access to a wide variety of markets, it contributes significantly to the customer benefit of end products, and it is difficult for competitors to imitate. Their tree metaphor makes the architecture visible: competencies are the roots, core products (components embodying competencies) the trunk, business units the branches, and end products the leaves. Judging a firm only by its end products \u2014 like judging a tree by its leaves \u2014 misses where strength actually resides.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is the competence tree: roots labelled with 2\u20133 core competencies, trunk as core products, branches as business units, leaves/fruit as end products. Teaching move: pick a diversified firm, have students fill the tree bottom-up, then test each named root against the three tests (market access, benefit contribution, imitability). Roots that fail get pruned from the drawing.",
    "examples": "1. Honda. One competence in engines and powertrains flowered into cars, motorcycles, marine engines, generators and lawnmowers \u2014 the canonical roots-and-branches story.\n2. 3M. Competence in adhesives, substrates and coatings yields tens of thousands of products from Post-its to medical tapes \u2014 competence, not category, defines the firm.\n3. Canon. Optics, imaging and microprocessor controls recombine across cameras, copiers, printers and lithography \u2014 Prahalad & Hamel's own flagship example.\n4. Titan. Precision engineering plus jewellery-retail trust extended from watches to eyewear and jewellery (Tanishq) \u2014 an Indian roots-to-branches case.\n5. Nokia (rigidity). Hardware and supply-chain mastery became the trap when advantage shifted to software ecosystems \u2014 competencies age.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Deciding what should hold a diversified group together and where group capital compounds best\n\u2022 Screening a proposed diversification for genuine capability leverage rather than empire-building\n\u2022 Choosing which capabilities to protect and never outsource\n\u2022 Guiding post-merger integration on which competencies to preserve\n\u2022 Redirecting R&D and talent investment toward the firm's distinctive strengths",
    "when_not": "19\n\u2022 In young companies still discovering what they are good at \u2014 competencies are built over years, not declared\n\u2022 To justify unrelated diversification \u2014 if the competence does not genuinely transfer, the tree metaphor misleads\n\u2022 When the claimed competence fails any of the three tests \u2014 most 'competencies' are merely competences (things done adequately)\n\u2022 As a reason never to outsource anything \u2014 the discipline applies to the competence, not every activity around it",
    "sources": "\u2022 Prahalad, C. K. & Hamel, G. (1990). 'The Core Competence of the Corporation.' Harvard Business Review, 68(3), 79\u201391.\n\u2022 Leonard-Barton, D. (1992). 'Core Capabilities and Core Rigidities.' Strategic Management Journal, 13(S1), 111\u2013125 \u2014 the 'core rigidity' counterpoint."
  },
  {
    "id": 9,
    "slug": "fw-09",
    "title": "BCG Growth\u2013Share Matrix",
    "subtitle": "Allocating resources across a business portfolio",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "The Boston Consulting Group matrix maps each business unit or product line on two axes: market growth rate (a proxy for cash needs and industry attractiveness) and relative market share (a proxy for cash generation, via experience-curve and scale effects). The four cells prescribe portfolio roles: Stars (high/high) need investment to hold leadership; Cash Cows (low growth, high share) generate surplus cash to be harvested; Question Marks (high growth, low share) demand a decisive invest-or-divest call; Dogs (low/low) are candidates for divestment or niche repositioning. The strategic logic is cash balance across the portfolio: cows fund question marks chosen to become stars, which become the next cows.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a 2\u00d72 with market growth rate on the vertical axis (high/low) and relative market share on the horizontal axis, conventionally drawn high-to-low so Stars sit top-left beside Question Marks top-right, Cash Cows bottom-left, Dogs bottom-right. Bubbles sized by revenue mark each business. Draw the cash-flow arrows \u2014 cows to question marks, question marks to stars \u2014 because the arrows, not the labels, carry the strategy.",
    "examples": "1. ITC. Cigarettes are the archetypal cash cow funding question marks in FMCG foods and personal care \u2014 some of which (Aashirvaad, Sunfeast) have become stars.\n2. Apple's line-up. iPhone functions as star-turned-cow funding services and Vision Pro (question marks), while legacy products (iPod) were retired as dogs.\n3. Unilever/HUL brand portfolio. Dozens of brands managed explicitly by portfolio role; periodic pruning (spreads divestment) shows harvest-and-exit discipline.\n4. Microsoft under Nadella. Windows managed as a cow while cash flooded into Azure \u2014 a question mark that became the defining star; the matrix explains the reallocation cleanly.\n5. Legacy media. Print businesses run as cows funding streaming question marks (e.g., Disney+) illustrate the model \u2014 and the risk when the cow declines faster than expected.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Allocating capital and management attention across a multi-business or multi-brand portfolio\n\u2022 Running a portfolio review to decide where to invest, hold, harvest or divest\n\u2022 Rationalising an over-extended product line\n\u2022 Communicating portfolio logic simply to a board or to investors\n\u2022 Planning the cash flow that lets cash cows fund tomorrow's growth bets",
    "when_not": "21\n\u2022 Where the share\u2013profitability link is weak \u2014 network-effect and platform businesses break the experience-curve assumption\n\u2022 For single-business companies \u2014 a portfolio tool needs a portfolio\n\u2022 When units share brands, costs or channels \u2014 mechanical divestment of 'dogs' can damage linked businesses\n\u2022 As a growth-forecasting tool \u2014 the axes describe today's position, not tomorrow's trajectory",
    "sources": "\u2022 Henderson, B. D. (1970). 'The Product Portfolio.' BCG Perspectives No. 66, Boston Consulting Group.\n\u2022 Reeves, M., Moose, S. & Venema, T. (2014). 'BCG Classics Revisited: The Growth Share Matrix.' Boston Consulting Group."
  },
  {
    "id": 10,
    "slug": "fw-10",
    "title": "GE\u2013McKinsey Nine-Box Matrix",
    "subtitle": "Multi-factor portfolio prioritisation",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "Developed at General Electric with McKinsey to overcome the BCG matrix's single-variable axes, the nine-box matrix positions each business unit on two composite dimensions: industry attractiveness (market size, growth, profitability, structure, trends) and competitive strength of the unit (share, brand, cost position, capabilities, customer relationships). Each dimension is scored on weighted criteria and rated high, medium or low, producing nine cells. The cells map to three strategic postures: invest/grow in the top-right region (attractive industry, strong unit), selectively manage for earnings along the diagonal, and harvest or divest in the bottom-left region. Bubble size typically shows unit revenue or the market's size.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a 3\u00d73 grid: industry attractiveness (low\u2192high) on the vertical axis, competitive strength (weak\u2192strong) on the horizontal. Shade the three top-right cells 'Invest/Grow', the three diagonal cells 'Selectivity/Earnings', the three bottom-left 'Harvest/Divest'. In class, add weighted-score tables beside each axis so students see the machinery behind a unit's placement, then perturb one weight to show how placements move.",
    "examples": "1. GE itself. Decades of portfolio moves \u2014 exiting plastics and appliances, doubling down on aviation \u2014 were argued through the nine-box logic before the eventual break-up.\n2. Mahindra Group. A diversified Indian group weighing autos, IT, hospitality and aerospace uses exactly this multi- criteria logic to decide where group capital compounds best.\n3. Siemens' portfolio surgery. Systematic exits (mobile phones, appliances, energy spin-off) with reinvestment into automation and healthcare tracks nine-box prescriptions almost literally.\n4. Reliance Industries. Scoring telecom (pre-Jio) as high-attractiveness/buildable-strength justified a bet that BCG-style share logic would have rejected \u2014 good for showing the frameworks disagree.\n5. P&G's brand cull. Selling ~100 brands to focus on ~65 category leaders is nine-box discipline applied to brands rather than business units.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Prioritising investment across many business units when two variables are too crude\n\u2022 Structuring a divestment debate around explicit, weighted criteria rather than opinion\n\u2022 Screening acquisition targets against the attractiveness\u2013strength profile of the existing portfolio\n\u2022 Reallocating capital in a conglomerate undergoing portfolio surgery\n\u2022 Deciding whether a strong unit in an attractive industry still belongs under this parent",
    "when_not": "23\n\u2022 When there is no discipline around scoring \u2014 weights chosen to protect favoured units make the grid theatre\n\u2022 For quick decisions \u2014 the method's data and judgement requirements are heavy; use BCG for a first cut\n\u2022 With fewer than a handful of business units \u2014 the machinery outweighs the insight\n\u2022 As a substitute for parenting logic \u2014 a well-placed unit may still create more value under a different owner",
    "sources": "\u2022 Developed by McKinsey & Company for General Electric in the early 1970s; no single canonical paper.\n\u2022 Hax, A. C. & Majluf, N. S. (1984). Strategic Management: An Integrative Perspective. Prentice Hall \u2014 documents the GE/McKinsey nine-box (industry attractiveness \u00d7 business strength) matrix."
  },
  {
    "id": 11,
    "slug": "fw-11",
    "title": "Ansoff Matrix",
    "subtitle": "Choosing a growth direction",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "Igor Ansoff's matrix (1957) organises growth options by two questions: are we selling existing or new products, and to existing or new markets? The combinations yield four strategies of rising risk. Market penetration: sell more of current products to current markets (share gain, usage frequency). Market development: take current products to new geographies, segments or channels. Product development: create new products for the customers you already know. Diversification: new products for new markets \u2014 the riskiest cell, justified only by strong synergy or attractive economics. The matrix disciplines growth conversations by making the risk gradient explicit: each step away from what the firm knows compounds uncertainty.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a 2\u00d72: Products (existing/new) across the top, Markets (existing/new) down the side, giving Market Penetration (existing/existing), Product Development (new product/existing market), Market Development (existing product/new market) and Diversification (new/new). Add a risk arrow running diagonally from penetration to diversification \u2014 that arrow is the framework's real content.",
    "examples": "1. Asian Paints. Decades of penetration and market development in coatings, then adjacent product development (waterproofing), and now diversification into home d\u00e9cor \u2014 a textbook risk-sequenced path.\n2. Netflix. Penetration (DVD subscribers), market development (international), product development (originals), diversification (games) \u2014 one company traversing all four cells in order.\n3. Amul beyond butter. Product development (cheese, ice cream, beverages) on an existing brand and distribution spine \u2014 synergy makes 'new product' moves low-risk.\n4. Byju's (cautionary). Aggressive simultaneous market and product diversification, largely by acquisition, without integration capability \u2014 a live case of the diagonal's dangers.\n5. Apple Watch. Arguably product development sold to existing iPhone owners \u2014 the ecosystem converts what looks like diversification into a lower-risk cell.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Structuring a growth-strategy discussion or the annual growth plan\n\u2022 Assessing the risk profile of a proposed expansion before funding it\n\u2022 Sequencing growth moves deliberately from lower- to higher-risk cells\n\u2022 Challenging a diversification proposal to make its synergy logic explicit\n\u2022 Rebalancing a growth portfolio that over-relies on one risky cell",
    "when_not": "25\n\u2022 For choosing between options within one cell \u2014 the matrix ranks directions, not individual projects\n\u2022 When capability adjacency is ignored \u2014 the grid measures market/product newness, not whether skills stretch\n\u2022 In declining core markets where penetration is a trap \u2014 the safest-looking cell can be the worst bet\n\u2022 As a creativity tool \u2014 it organises growth ideas but does not generate them",
    "sources": "\u2022 Ansoff, H. I. (1957). 'Strategies for Diversification.' Harvard Business Review, 35(5), 113\u2013124 \u2014 the product/market growth matrix.\n\u2022 Ansoff, H. I. (1965). Corporate Strategy. McGraw-Hill."
  },
  {
    "id": 12,
    "slug": "fw-12",
    "title": "RASCI Responsibility Matrix",
    "subtitle": "Clarifying who does what in strategy execution (cross-cutting: GTM launches, campaigns)",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "RASCI is a responsibility-assignment matrix that removes ambiguity about roles in any initiative. For each task or decision, it assigns: Responsible \u2014 who does the work; Accountable \u2014 the single owner answerable for the outcome (exactly one per task); Supportive \u2014 who provides resources or assists execution; Consulted \u2014 whose input is sought before action (two-way communication); and Informed \u2014 who is told after decisions or completion (one-way). It extends the classic RACI by separating hands-on Support from advisory Consultation. Charted as a grid of tasks against roles, RASCI is the connective tissue between strategy and execution \u2014 strategies fail less from bad analysis than from unclear ownership. It applies equally to strategy rollouts, marketing campaigns and cross-functional go-to-market launches.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a matrix: tasks/deliverables as rows, roles/people as columns, cells carrying R, A, S, C or I. Highlight one row to show the rule set (one A, at least one R) and one column to show workload analysis. When teaching, colour-code the letters and run the column/row diagnostics aloud \u2014 the grid becomes an organisational X-ray rather than a form.",
    "examples": "1. Product launch. Launch plan tasks gridded against PM (often A), engineering (R), sales enablement (S), legal (C) and leadership (I) \u2014 the classic GTM RASCI that prevents day-one chaos.\n2. ERP implementation. A CIO accountable, system integrator responsible, process owners consulted, finance supportive, all-staff informed \u2014 clarity that determines whether the go-live holds.\n3. Marketing campaign. Brand manager A, agency R, media buying S, legal/regulatory C, country managers I \u2014 clarifies the agency relationship that briefs alone never do.\n4. Strategy cascade. Each strategic-plan initiative assigned an accountable executive with named R's in functions \u2014 turns an off-site document into an executable programme.\n5. Hospital discharge process. A non-business example: attending physician A, nurses R, pharmacy S, insurance desk C, family I \u2014 shows the grammar generalises.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Operationalising a strategic initiative across several functions\n\u2022 Coordinating a go-to-market launch spanning product, marketing, sales and support\n\u2022 Fixing chronic decision bottlenecks or duplicated work in a matrixed organisation\n\u2022 Clarifying ownership during post-merger integration or a reorganisation\n\u2022 Setting up governance for a large programme or transformation 27",
    "when_not": "\u2022 For creative or exploratory work where roles must stay fluid \u2014 premature role-fixing kills initiative\n\u2022 In very small teams where everyone genuinely does everything \u2014 the grid adds ceremony, not clarity\n\u2022 When leadership will not enforce the single-A rule \u2014 a RASCI with shared accountability documents confusion instead of removing it\n\u2022 As a substitute for competence or trust \u2014 clear roles cannot fix the wrong people in them",
    "sources": "\u2022 Cleland, D. I. & King, W. R. (1968). Systems Analysis and Project Management. McGraw-Hill \u2014 early linear responsibility charting, the ancestor of RACI/RASCI.\n\u2022 Project Management Institute (2021). A Guide to the Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK\u00ae Guide, 7th ed.). PMI \u2014 responsibility assignment matrix (RACI).\n\u2022 Note: RASCI is a widely used practitioner extension of RACI (separating 'Support' from 'Consulted') without a single canonical author."
  },
  {
    "id": 13,
    "slug": "fw-13",
    "title": "Marketing Mix: 4Ps / 7Ps",
    "subtitle": "The levers that execute a positioning",
    "part": "Part II \u2014 Marketing Strategy",
    "desc": "McCarthy's 4Ps organise the controllable levers of marketing execution: Product (features, quality, design, packaging, service, brand), Price (list price, discounts, terms \u2014 the only P that generates rather than spends revenue), Place (channels, coverage, logistics, inventory) and Promotion (advertising, sales force, PR, digital, sales promotion). Booms and Bitner extended the mix to 7Ps for services, adding People (staff who deliver the experience), Process (service delivery flows and queues) and Physical Evidence (tangible cues \u2014 environment, design, documents \u2014 that signal invisible quality.) The mix is not a checklist but a consistency test: every P must reinforce the same STP choices, and the Ps must cohere with each other \u2014 premium product with discount pricing signals confusion.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic shows the target customer at the centre with the four Ps as quadrants around it \u2014 Product, Price, Place, Promotion \u2014 and the three service Ps (People, Process, Physical Evidence) as an outer ring. The centre placement is deliberate pedagogy: every lever is set with reference to the chosen target, and the outer ring signals the service extension rather than a separate model.",
    "examples": "1. Apple. Premium product design, premium pricing, controlled distribution (own stores), aspirational promotion \u2014 four Ps locked in mutual reinforcement; discounting would damage the whole system.\n2. A quick-service restaurant. 7Ps in action: standardised menu (product), value pricing, high-street sites (place), staff scripts and uniforms (people), assembly-line kitchens (process), and cleanliness cues (physical evidence).\n3. Patanjali's rise. Ayurvedic product story, value pricing, rapid kirana distribution and founder-led promotion \u2014 a coherent mix that took share from multinationals in years.\n4. IKEA. Flat-pack product design enables the place economics (out-of-town warehouses) which enable the pricing \u2014 the Ps are engineered jointly, not chosen independently.\n5. Taj Hotels (7Ps). Legendary staff culture (people), rituals of arrival (process), architecture and scent (physical evidence) \u2014 the service Ps as the differentiator.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Translating STP decisions into an executable marketing programme\n\u2022 Auditing a struggling brand for internal inconsistencies across the levers\n\u2022 Designing the offer for a service business where people, process and evidence dominate\n\u2022 Building the action chapters of a marketing or launch plan\n\u2022 Reviewing whether pricing, product, channel and promotion still reinforce one another",
    "when_not": "\u2022 Before STP is done \u2014 mix decisions without a chosen target and positioning are levers pulled at random\n\u2022 As a customer-insight tool \u2014 the Ps are the seller's controls; use 4Cs or JTBD to see the buyer's side\n\u2022 When treated as four independent checklists \u2014 the mix only works as a mutually consistent system\n\u2022 In pure B2B relationship businesses where the sales process, not the promotional mix, drives outcomes \u2014 adapt heavily",
    "sources": "\u2022 Borden, N. H. (1964). 'The Concept of the Marketing Mix.' Journal of Advertising Research, 4(2), 2\u20137.\n\u2022 McCarthy, E. J. (1960). Basic Marketing: A Managerial Approach. Richard D. Irwin \u2014 the 4Ps (Product, Price, Place, Promotion).\n\u2022 Booms, B. H. & Bitner, M. J. (1981). 'Marketing Strategies and Organization Structures for Service Firms.' In Marketing of Services, AMA \u2014 the extended 7Ps for services."
  },
  {
    "id": 14,
    "slug": "fw-14",
    "title": "4Cs: The Customer-Centric Mix",
    "subtitle": "Reframing the 4Ps from the buyer's side",
    "part": "Part II \u2014 Marketing Strategy",
    "desc": "Robert Lauterborn's 4Cs (1990) re-states each P from the customer's perspective, on the argument that sellers' categories blind them to buyers' realities. Product becomes Customer value/solution: nobody wants a product, they want a problem solved. Price becomes Cost: the customer's total cost of ownership \u2014 money, time, effort, risk \u2014 not just the list price. Place becomes Convenience: how easily the customer can find, evaluate, buy and receive, in an era when channels are the customer's choice. Promotion becomes Communication: two-way dialogue and engagement rather than one-way persuasion. The 4Cs do not replace the 4Ps operationally \u2014 firms still set prices and choose channels \u2014 but they force each lever to be designed from the customer's chair.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic shows four paired transformations \u2014 Product\u2192Customer Value, Price\u2192Cost, Place\u2192Convenience, Promotion\u2192Communication \u2014 drawn as arrows from the seller's frame (left column) to the buyer's frame (right column). Teach it as a translation table: any 4P decision can be re-expressed in the right column, and discrepancies between the columns are marketing opportunities.",
    "examples": "1. Amazon. One-click ordering, Prime delivery and easy returns are Convenience and Cost (time, risk) innovations \u2014 its dominance owes more to the Cs than to product or price alone.\n2. Electric vehicles. EV marketing that leads with total cost of ownership (fuel and maintenance savings) over sticker price is a deliberate Price\u2192Cost reframe.\n3. Zerodha. Discount pricing mattered, but radical simplification of the account-opening and trading experience (Convenience, learning Cost) built India's largest broker with near-zero advertising.\n4. Nespresso. High per-cup price but minimal effort, mess and decision cost \u2014 winning on the customer's full cost equation, not the price tag.\n5. SaaS free trials. Replacing persuasion with hands-on experience converts Promotion into Communication and lowers the customer's risk cost simultaneously.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Auditing a seller-centric marketing plan for customer blind spots\n\u2022 Designing digital and e-commerce strategy where convenience and dialogue decide outcomes\n\u2022 Building a total-cost-of-ownership argument in a B2B sale\n\u2022 Translating customer-experience insight into concrete marketing-mix decisions\n\u2022 Repositioning a product to compete on the customer's full cost equation, not just price 31",
    "when_not": "\u2022 As a replacement for the 4Ps in planning \u2014 firms still must set prices and pick channels; the Cs reframe, they do not operationalise\n\u2022 When total-cost or convenience data on customers is unavailable \u2014 the Cs then become slogans\n\u2022 In commodity markets where price genuinely is the only C that matters to buyers\n\u2022 As a communications-only exercise \u2014 renaming Promotion 'Communication' changes nothing without two-way mechanics behind it",
    "sources": "\u2022 Lauterborn, R. F. (1990). 'New Marketing Litany: Four Ps Pass\u00e9; C-Words Take Over.' Advertising Age, 61(41), 26 \u2014 the 4Cs (Customer value, Cost, Convenience, Communication)."
  },
  {
    "id": 15,
    "slug": "fw-15",
    "title": "STP: Segmentation, Targeting, Positioning",
    "subtitle": "The backbone of marketing strategy",
    "part": "Part II \u2014 Marketing Strategy",
    "desc": "STP is the strategic core of marketing. Segmentation divides a heterogeneous market into groups with similar needs and responses, using demographic, geographic, psychographic, behavioural or needs-based variables \u2014 good segments are measurable, substantial, accessible, differentiable and actionable. Targeting evaluates segment attractiveness (size, growth, competition, fit with capabilities) and chooses which to serve \u2014 concentrated, selective, differentiated or mass approaches. Positioning designs the offer and image to occupy a distinctive, valued place in the target customer's mind relative to competitors, usually crystallised in a positioning statement: for (target), (brand) is the (frame of reference) that (point of difference) because (reason to believe). Everything downstream \u2014 product, price, channels, communication \u2014 should be derivable from the STP choices.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a three-step arrow flow: Segmentation (market split into distinct groups) \u2192 Targeting (evaluation and selection of segments) \u2192 Positioning (designing the value proposition and perceptual place for chosen segments). Under each step list its key questions and criteria. Teach it left to right, but note that in practice firms iterate \u2014 a positioning insight can redefine the segmentation.",
    "examples": "1. Maruti vs Mercedes in India. Same industry, opposite STP: Maruti targets value-seeking first-time buyers positioned on affordability and service reach; Mercedes targets affluent achievers positioned on prestige and engineering.\n2. Dove. Segmented beauty by attitude rather than demographics, targeted women alienated by idealised imagery, and positioned on 'real beauty' \u2014 an attitudinal STP that sustained decades of growth.\n3. Nike vs New Balance. Nike targets aspiration-driven athletes ('everyone with a body'), New Balance targets comfort- and-craft seekers \u2014 same products physically, different minds occupied.\n4. Indigo vs Air India post-privatisation. Indigo holds punctuality-value positioning; Air India is attempting repositioning toward premium full-service \u2014 a live repositioning case.\n5. Slack's early STP. Targeted software teams (not 'all businesses'), positioned against email ('be less busy') \u2014 beachhead targeting linking to GTM.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Setting the foundational architecture of a marketing plan or brand launch\n\u2022 Entering a new market and deciding whom to serve first and how to be perceived\n\u2022 Repositioning a tired brand or responding to a new competitor\n\u2022 Managing multiple brands so each occupies a distinct position in one category\n\u2022 Reallocating marketing spend toward the most attractive, winnable segments 33",
    "when_not": "\u2022 In tiny or nascent markets where slicing demand further produces segments too small to serve economically\n\u2022 When data cannot distinguish segments behaviourally \u2014 demographic labels without behavioural difference are not segments\n\u2022 For True mass commodities (grid electricity, basic staples) where needs are genuinely homogeneous\n\u2022 When the organisation cannot act differently by segment \u2014 segmentation without differentiated action is analysis for its own sake",
    "sources": "\u2022 Smith, W. R. (1956). 'Product Differentiation and Market Segmentation as Alternative Marketing Strategies.' Journal of Marketing, 21(1), 3\u20138.\n\u2022 Kotler, P. & Keller, K. L. (2016). Marketing Management (15th ed.). Pearson \u2014 the modern Segmentation\u2013Targeting\u2013Positioning formulation."
  },
  {
    "id": 16,
    "slug": "fw-16",
    "title": "Perceptual / Positioning Maps",
    "subtitle": "Visualising how customers see the competitive field",
    "part": "Part II \u2014 Marketing Strategy",
    "desc": "A perceptual map plots brands in the customer's mind: two (or more) attribute dimensions that drive choice \u2014 price vs quality, convenience vs experience, performance vs sustainability \u2014 with each brand located by customer perception data (surveys, similarity ratings, multidimensional scaling), not by managerial belief. Ideal points or preference vectors can be overlaid to show where demand concentrates. The map answers three positioning questions at a glance: who are our real perceptual competitors (proximity, regardless of industry definitions), is our intended positioning actually perceived (strategy\u2013perception gaps), and where are the open spaces \u2014 under-served combinations of attributes that could support repositioning or new entry?",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a two-axis map (e.g., Price: low\u2194high on x; Quality/Experience: basic\u2194premium on y) with labelled brand points scattered across quadrants, a dashed circle marking an open 'white space', and one brand shown with an arrow indicating intended repositioning. Emphasise in teaching that every coordinate must come from customer data \u2014 the moment managers place the dots themselves, the tool becomes a mirror of their assumptions.",
    "examples": "1. Automobile brands. Mapping economy\u2194luxury against practical\u2194sporty shows Toyota and BMW barely compete perceptually \u2014 and revealed the gap Lexus was engineered to fill.\n2. Indian coffee retail. Price vs experience mapping shows Starbucks, Third Wave and udupi caf\u00e9s occupying distinct territories \u2014 and where new entrants like Blue Tokai found space.\n3. Beer brands. The classic classroom dataset: mass vs craft and light vs full-bodied dimensions expose the premiumisation white space craft brewers filled.\n4. B-schools. Mapping rigour vs industry-orientation and global vs regional lets students apply the tool to institutions they know intimately \u2014 including their own.\n5. Airlines after repositioning. Before/after maps of Air India's transformation attempt make perceptual inertia measurable \u2014 perceptions move years behind product changes.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Making a positioning choice visible and testable with customer data\n\u2022 Hunting for white-space opportunities for a new product or a reposition\n\u2022 Tracking whether a campaign actually shifted customer perceptions over time\n\u2022 Diagnosing unwanted convergence with a competitor (brand drift)\n\u2022 Briefing agencies and the board on where the brand sits versus rivals",
    "when_not": "35\n\u2022 When manager opinion, not customer data, places the dots \u2014 the map then mirrors internal bias\n\u2022 For low-awareness categories or new brands customers cannot yet rate\n\u2022 When more than two attributes genuinely drive choice and flattening to two axes distorts the space \u2014 use multiple maps\n\u2022 To find 'gaps' without demand evidence \u2014 an empty quadrant may be empty because nobody wants that combination",
    "sources": "\u2022 Ries, A. & Trout, J. (1981). Positioning: The Battle for Your Mind. McGraw-Hill.\n\u2022 Green, P. E. & Rao, V. R. (1972). Applied Multidimensional Scaling. Holt, Rinehart & Winston \u2014 the perceptual-mapping methodology."
  },
  {
    "id": 17,
    "slug": "fw-17",
    "title": "Product Life Cycle",
    "subtitle": "Adapting strategy as products age",
    "part": "Part II \u2014 Marketing Strategy",
    "desc": "The Product Life Cycle models a product category's sales and profits through four stages. Introduction: slow sales, heavy investment, innovator customers, negative profits \u2014 priorities are awareness and trial. Growth: accelerating adoption, rising profits, entering competitors \u2014 priorities are share capture, distribution expansion and product improvement. Maturity: sales plateau, competition intensifies, margins compress \u2014 priorities shift to differentiation, segmentation, cost control and usage expansion. Decline: falling sales as needs or technologies shift \u2014 choices are harvest, niche defence, or divest. Each stage prescribes different marketing-mix emphases, making the PLC a scheduling device for strategy change \u2014 the mistake it warns against is managing a mature product with growth-stage tactics, or vice versa.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic plots sales (solid curve) and profit (dashed curve, lagging and lower) against time across four labelled bands \u2014 Introduction, Growth, Maturity, Decline. Profit turns positive during growth and peaks before sales do, in maturity's early phase: that lead-lag is the exam-worthy detail. Annotate each band with its strategic priority (trial \u2192 share \u2192 defend/segment \u2192 harvest/exit).",
    "examples": "1. Smartphones. A category traversing the full curve in fifteen years: breathless growth, then maturity visible in lengthening replacement cycles, incremental features and price segmentation.\n2. Vinyl records. Decline is not always terminal: vinyl's niche revival shows a decline-stage product repositioned around ritual and authenticity.\n3. Colas. Deep maturity managed masterfully: endless flavour/packaging segmentation, usage-occasion expansion and marketing spend as a share war, not a growth engine.\n4. Digital cameras. A compressed lifecycle: explosive growth, abrupt smartphone-driven decline within a decade \u2014 technology substitution can amputate the curve.\n5. UPI payments. Growth stage in real time: network expansion, feature competition and land-grab economics ahead of eventual monetisation maturity.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Setting stage-appropriate objectives, budgets and mix decisions for a product\n\u2022 Timing the portfolio so new products ramp as existing ones mature\n\u2022 Forecasting competitive intensity and margin trajectory for a category\n\u2022 Deciding when to harvest, reposition or exit a declining line\n\u2022 Justifying continued investment (or disinvestment) to finance and the board 37",
    "when_not": "\u2022 For individual brands \u2014 the curve describes categories; strong brands grow in mature categories routinely\n\u2022 As a forecast \u2014 stages are clear only in hindsight, and declaring decline can become self-fulfilling\n\u2022 In categories subject to abrupt technological substitution, where the curve amputates rather than tapers\n\u2022 To time exits mechanically \u2014 revival (vinyl, fountain pens) shows 'decline' can be a repositioning opportunity",
    "sources": "\u2022 Levitt, T. (1965). 'Exploit the Product Life Cycle.' Harvard Business Review, 43(6), 81\u201394.\n\u2022 Dean, J. (1950). 'Pricing Policies for New Products.' Harvard Business Review, 28(6), 45\u201353 \u2014 early life-cycle pricing."
  },
  {
    "id": 18,
    "slug": "fw-18",
    "title": "Kano Model",
    "subtitle": "Not all features move satisfaction equally",
    "part": "Part II \u2014 Marketing Strategy",
    "desc": "Noriaki Kano's model classifies product attributes by how their performance affects customer satisfaction. Must-be (basic) attributes cause dissatisfaction when absent but no delight when present \u2014 brakes, hygiene, uptime. One- dimensional (performance) attributes drive satisfaction linearly \u2014 battery life, mileage, speed: more is better and customers articulate them readily. Attractive (delighter) attributes create disproportionate delight when present but no complaint when absent \u2014 the unexpected touch. Two further classes matter: indifferent attributes (nobody cares) and reverse attributes (some customers actively dislike). Two dynamics give the model bite: delight decays \u2014 yesterday's delighter becomes today's must-be (hotel Wi-Fi) \u2014 and customers cannot articulate delighters in research, since they don't know they want them.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic plots implementation/performance (x-axis: absent\u2192fully implemented) against customer satisfaction (y-axis: dissatisfied\u2192delighted). Three curves: Must-be rising steeply from deep dissatisfaction but plateauing at neutral; Performance as a straight diagonal through the origin; Attractive rising from neutral into delight with an accelerating curve. Add a downward arrow labelled 'delight decays over time' showing attractive attributes migrating toward must-be \u2014 the dynamic is the model's most strategic element.",
    "examples": "1. Hotel room. Clean sheets are must-be, room size is performance, a handwritten welcome note is a delighter \u2014 and free Wi-Fi has migrated from delighter (2005) to must-be (today).\n2. Smartphone cameras. Once delighters, then the key performance attribute, now approaching must-be parity \u2014 a full Kano migration in fifteen years.\n3. Airline economy class. Safety is must-be, legroom is performance, the surprise upgrade is the delighter \u2014 and low- cost carriers profitably strip everything except must-bes and price.\n4. Tesla's early delighters. Over-the-air updates and ludicrous mode were pure delighters that defined the brand; rivals' catch-up is converting them to performance attributes.\n5. Banking apps. Instant UPI is now must-be in India; spend analytics are performance; a proactive fraud call that saves a customer is the delighter that earns advocacy.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Prioritising features on a product roadmap under limited engineering capacity\n\u2022 Allocating quality investment \u2014 fixing basics before adding delighters\n\u2022 Interpreting customer research that keeps missing what actually delights\n\u2022 Choosing which attribute class to compete on for differentiation\n\u2022 Deciding whether to match a rival's new feature or let it become table stakes 39",
    "when_not": "\u2022 Without primary research \u2014 guessing which attributes delight produces confident, wrong roadmaps\n\u2022 When one aggregate analysis is run across segments with opposite preferences \u2014 one group's delighter is another's annoyance\n\u2022 For long-cycle products where delight decay outpaces development \u2014 yesterday's classification ships as today's must-be\n\u2022 As a pricing tool \u2014 Kano maps satisfaction, not willingness to pay",
    "sources": "\u2022 Kano, N., Seraku, N., Takahashi, F. & Tsuji, S. (1984). 'Attractive Quality and Must-Be Quality.' Journal of the Japanese Society for Quality Control, 14(2), 39\u201348."
  },
  {
    "id": 19,
    "slug": "fw-19",
    "title": "Customer Journey & the AIDA Funnel",
    "subtitle": "Mapping the path from awareness to advocacy",
    "part": "Part II \u2014 Marketing Strategy",
    "desc": "AIDA \u2014 Attention (or Awareness), Interest, Desire, Action \u2014 is the oldest model of how communication moves buyers (St Elmo Lewis, 1898), and the ancestor of every marketing funnel. The funnel shape encodes attrition: many become aware, fewer grow interested, fewer still desire, and a fraction act \u2014 so marketing's job is both filling the top and fixing the leaks. Modern extensions add post-purchase stages (retention, loyalty, advocacy), turning the funnel into a loop where advocates refill the top. Customer-journey mapping enriches the funnel with the customer's actual experience: touchpoints, channels, emotions, and pain points at each stage \u2014 recognising that real paths are nonlinear, multi-device and socially influenced rather than a tidy slide downward.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a four-band funnel \u2014 Attention, Interest, Desire, Action \u2014 narrowing downward, with stage-conversion arrows on one side and a loyalty/advocacy loop drawn from the funnel's exit back to the top. Annotate each band with a sample metric and a typical marketing instrument. The loop matters as much as the funnel: it converts a one-shot model into a compounding one.",
    "examples": "1. E-commerce funnel. Ad impression \u2192 site visit \u2192 product page \u2192 cart \u2192 purchase, with measured drop-offs at each step; cart-abandonment email flows are leak-fixing made literal.\n2. Automobile purchase journey. Months of research, configurator visits, YouTube reviews, showroom test drive, negotiation \u2014 a journey map exposes how little of the decision the dealer actually sees.\n3. B2B enterprise sales. MQL\u2192SQL\u2192opportunity\u2192closed-won is AIDA in CRM clothing; stage-conversion benchmarks drive the entire revenue-operations discipline.\n4. Film marketing. Trailer (attention) \u2192 social buzz (interest) \u2192 reviews and booking apps (desire) \u2192 opening weekend (action) \u2014 with advocacy determining week two.\n5. Insurance in India. High awareness, low action: the funnel diagnosis points to trust and complexity barriers at the desire stage \u2014 hence agent-led and simplified digital models.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Structuring communication objectives and budgets by stage\n\u2022 Diagnosing where conversion breaks down using stage-to-stage data\n\u2022 Designing content and channel plans matched to each stage's mindset\n\u2022 Aligning marketing and sales in a B2B pipeline (MQL to closed-won)\n\u2022 Building the business case for retention and advocacy, not just acquisition 41",
    "when_not": "\u2022 As a model of actual buyer behaviour \u2014 real journeys loop and skip stages; the funnel is a management accounting device\n\u2022 In high-trust referral businesses where advocacy, not advertising, starts the journey \u2014 the loop matters more than the funnel\n\u2022 When each stage lacks a defined metric and owner \u2014 an unmeasured funnel is a drawing\n\u2022 For one-off distress purchases (emergency repairs) where deliberation stages barely exist",
    "sources": "\u2022 Lewis, E. St. Elmo (attrib., 1898\u20131903) \u2014 the Attention\u2013Interest\u2013Desire\u2013Action sequence.\n\u2022 Strong, E. K. (1925). The Psychology of Selling and Advertising. McGraw-Hill \u2014 first documented formalisation of AIDA.\n\u2022 Court, D., Elzinga, D., Mulder, S. & Vetvik, O. J. (2009). 'The Consumer Decision Journey.' McKinsey Quarterly \u2014 the modern loop critique."
  },
  {
    "id": 20,
    "slug": "fw-20",
    "title": "TAM / SAM / SOM",
    "subtitle": "Sizing the market honestly",
    "part": "Part III \u2014 Go-to-Market Strategy",
    "desc": "Market sizing proceeds through three nested estimates. TAM (Total Addressable Market): the full revenue opportunity if every potential customer of the product category were captured \u2014 the outer bound. SAM (Serviceable Addressable Market): the portion of TAM reachable with your actual product, geography, channel and regulatory scope. SOM (Serviceable Obtainable Market): the share of SAM realistically winnable in a planning horizon given competition, capacity and go-to-market strength \u2014 the number a revenue plan should rest on. Two estimation routes should triangulate: top-down (industry reports sliced by filters) and bottom-up (number of target customers \u00d7 realistic price \u00d7 plausible penetration). Bottom-up is more credible because it exposes assumptions; top- down alone invites the '1% of a huge market' fallacy.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is three concentric circles: TAM outermost, SAM inside it, SOM at the core, each labelled with its definition and its typical estimation route. Annotate the rings with the filters that shrink each level (product scope, geography, channel \u2192 competition, capacity, time). In class, attach real numbers from a case and show the shrinkage ratios \u2014 students are consistently surprised how small credible SOM is relative to headline TAM.",
    "examples": "1. Food delivery in India. TAM: all food-away-from-home spend. SAM: online-ordering households in serviceable cities. SOM: winnable share given Zomato/Swiggy's entrenchment \u2014 orders of magnitude apart, and only SOM funds a business plan.\n2. A B2B SaaS startup. TAM: global HR software. SAM: mid-market companies in English-speaking markets needing payroll compliance. SOM: what 20 salespeople can close in 3 years \u2014 bottom-up from quota arithmetic.\n3. Uber's TAM error (instructive). Early analyses sized it against the taxi market (~$4B US); the product expanded the market itself \u2014 category-creating products need behaviour-based, not spend-based, sizing.\n4. OYO. TAM by hotel-room spend flattered the story; SAM shrank sharply once quality-controllable properties and viable cities were filtered \u2014 a live lesson in the TAM\u2192SAM discount.\n5. EV charging networks. TAM depends on EV-fleet projections \u2014 sizing that requires scenario ranges, not point estimates.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Sizing the opportunity for a venture pitch or an investment case\n\u2022 Deciding whether a new-market entry is worth the cost of entry\n\u2022 Prioritising among segments or geographies by obtainable value\n\u2022 Sanity-checking a revenue plan against market arithmetic\n\u2022 Auditing a founder's or business unit's sizing claims before funding",
    "when_not": "\u2022 As proof of venture quality \u2014 a big TAM says nothing about the team's ability to capture any of it\n\u2022 For category-creating products sized off existing spend \u2014 behaviour-based sizing is required, or the estimate is meaningless\n\u2022 When only a top-down number is quoted \u2014 without a bottom-up reconciliation the figure is a citation, not an analysis\n\u2022 In fast-changing markets treated statically \u2014 TAM in year five, not today, is the decision-relevant number",
    "sources": "\u2022 Blank, S. (2005). The Four Steps to the Epiphany. Cafepress/K&S Ranch \u2014 market-sizing within customer development.\n\u2022 Mullins, J. (2006). The New Business Road Test. FT Prentice Hall \u2014 top-down vs bottom-up market sizing.\n\u2022 Note: TAM/SAM/SOM is standard venture-finance practice with no single canonical academic origin."
  },
  {
    "id": 21,
    "slug": "fw-21",
    "title": "Product\u2013Market Fit",
    "subtitle": "Validate demand before you scale",
    "part": "Part III \u2014 Go-to-Market Strategy",
    "desc": "Product\u2013market fit (Marc Andreessen's term) is the state of being in a good market with a product that satisfies it \u2014 the point where the market pulls the product out of you rather than you pushing it in. Pre-PMF, the company's only job is finding fit: iterating on the value proposition against a defined segment. Post-PMF, the job flips to scaling \u2014 pouring resources into growth engines that now have something worth amplifying. Scaling before fit is the leading cause of startup death: it multiplies burn against a proposition customers don't yet want. Fit is measured by proxies: Sean Ellis's threshold (>40% of users 'very disappointed' if the product vanished), retention curves that flatten rather than decay to zero, high organic/word-of-mouth share of growth, and improving cohort economics.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a two-layer pyramid/gate: the lower layer 'Find Product\u2013Market Fit' (iterate: value proposition \u2194 target segment, measured by retention, Ellis score, organic growth) with a gate labelled 'evidence of fit' leading to the upper layer 'Scale' (channels, sales, spend). A side panel sketches two cohort-retention curves \u2014 one decaying to zero (no fit), one flattening (fit) \u2014 the single most useful picture in the topic.",
    "examples": "1. Airbnb's grind to fit. Years of iteration \u2014 professional photography, trust mechanisms, host tools \u2014 before retention and organic growth signalled fit; scaling followed, not preceded, those signals.\n2. Quibi (cautionary). $1.75B raised and spent on content and marketing before any evidence customers wanted short premium mobile video \u2014 scale-before-fit in its purest form.\n3. Slack. Explosive organic team-to-team spread and daily-use retention were fit signals visible long before the sales motion existed.\n4. Indian quick-commerce. Contested case for debate: strong retention in dense metros (fit for a segment) but unit economics questioned \u2014 is that PMF or subsidised habit?\n5. Instagram's pivot. Burbn had no fit; its photo-filter feature did \u2014 finding fit sometimes means shrinking the product to the piece that retains.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Deciding whether a venture should still be searching for fit or is ready to scale\n\u2022 Distinguishing real traction from paid-growth illusion in an investment decision\n\u2022 Gating a new product's rollout inside an established firm (fit before scale)\n\u2022 Diagnosing stalled growth to test whether fit was ever truly achieved\n\u2022 Setting the metrics (retention, Ellis score, organic share) a team will steer by 45",
    "when_not": "\u2022 For businesses with inherently long feedback cycles (infrastructure, deep tech, pharma) where retention signals arrive years late\n\u2022 When growth is entirely paid \u2014 spend can simulate every fit signal except retention; do not read momentum as fit\n\u2022 As a permanent state \u2014 fit erodes when markets shift; past fit does not license present scaling\n\u2022 At aggregate level in multi-segment products \u2014 strong fit in one niche can hide zero fit everywhere else",
    "sources": "\u2022 Andreessen, M. (2007). 'The Pmarca Guide to Startups, Part 4: The Only Thing That Matters.' blog.pmarca.com \u2014 popularised 'product/market fit'.\n\u2022 Rachleff, A. \u2014 credited by Andreessen with the underlying concept; see also Ellis, S. (2010) for the '40% very disappointed' survey test."
  },
  {
    "id": 22,
    "slug": "fw-22",
    "title": "Crossing the Chasm (Moore)",
    "subtitle": "From early adopters to the mainstream",
    "part": "Part III \u2014 Go-to-Market Strategy",
    "desc": "Geoffrey Moore's insight is that the technology adoption curve is not smooth: a chasm separates early adopters \u2014 visionaries who buy revolutionary potential and tolerate rough edges \u2014 from the early majority: pragmatists who buy proven, complete solutions with references from other pragmatists. Marketing that wins visionaries (vision, novelty, disruption) actively repels pragmatists, so ventures die in the chasm with strong early revenue and no mainstream traction. Moore's crossing strategy is deliberately narrow: pick one beachhead segment ('big fish, small pond'), assemble the whole product \u2014 everything the pragmatist needs for a complete solution, including integrations, support and services \u2014 dominate that niche to generate pragmatist references, then expand segment by segment (the bowling alley).",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is the adoption bell curve \u2014 Innovators, Early Adopters, Early Majority, Late Majority, Laggards \u2014 with a visible gap (the chasm) cut between Early Adopters and Early Majority, and an arrow vaulting it labelled with the crossing strategy: beachhead niche + whole product + pragmatist references. Annotate each side of the gap with its buying psychology (vision/first vs proof/safe) \u2014 the psychological contrast is what makes the picture teach.",
    "examples": "1. Documentum (Moore's classic). Chose pharmaceutical regulatory-affairs departments as its beachhead \u2014 a small, desperate niche \u2014 dominated it, then expanded bowling-pin by bowling-pin into adjacent regulated document markets.\n2. Tesla's sequence. Roadster for visionaries, Model S for affluent pragmatists with charging infrastructure as whole product, then Model 3 mainstream \u2014 a staged chasm crossing over 15 years.\n3. Salesforce. Early SMB sales-team beachhead with 'no software' whole-product simplicity, then methodical march up- market \u2014 chasm discipline in SaaS form.\n4. VR headsets (in the chasm). A decade of enthusiast adoption without pragmatist crossing: incomplete whole product (content, comfort, use-case) keeps the mainstream waiting \u2014 a live negative example.\n5. UPI (contrast). Crossed to the mainstream unusually fast because the whole product (interoperability, zero cost, ubiquitous acceptance) was engineered publicly from day one.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Diagnosing why a technology product stalled after strong early-adopter sales\n\u2022 Sequencing the launch of a discontinuous or category-new product\n\u2022 Choosing the beachhead segment and assembling its whole product\n\u2022 Designing a reference-based selling motion to win pragmatist buyers\n\u2022 Deciding when to broaden the market and when to stay narrow 47",
    "when_not": "\u2022 For continuous, low-risk innovations \u2014 cheaper snacks or incremental app features face no pragmatist adoption barrier\n\u2022 In markets where trial is free and instant \u2014 freemium can shrink the chasm to near zero; check before prescribing niche focus\n\u2022 When early sales come from pragmatists already \u2014 some B2B categories have no visionary segment to speak of\n\u2022 As an excuse for stalled growth that is actually a product or pricing problem \u2014 not every plateau is a chasm",
    "sources": "\u2022 Moore, G. A. (1991). Crossing the Chasm: Marketing and Selling High-Tech Products to Mainstream Customers. HarperBusiness.\n\u2022 Moore, G. A. (2014). Crossing the Chasm (3rd ed.). HarperBusiness."
  },
  {
    "id": 23,
    "slug": "fw-23",
    "title": "Diffusion of Innovations (Rogers)",
    "subtitle": "How adoption spreads through a population",
    "part": "Part III \u2014 Go-to-Market Strategy",
    "desc": "Everett Rogers (1962) showed innovations spread through social systems in a predictable pattern: adopters distribute roughly normally across five categories \u2014 Innovators (2.5%, risk-seeking experimenters), Early Adopters (13.5%, respected opinion leaders), Early Majority (34%, deliberate pragmatists), Late Majority (34%, sceptics adopting under pressure), and Laggards (16%, tradition-bound). Cumulative adoption traces an S-curve: slow start, steep middle as social proof compounds, saturation. Adoption speed is governed by five perceived attributes of the innovation: relative advantage (better than what it replaces), compatibility (with values, habits, infrastructure), complexity (harder = slower), trialability (can it be tested cheaply?), and observability (are benefits visible to others?). These five are levers marketers can design against, not just descriptors.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic overlays two curves: the bell curve of adopter categories (with the five segments labelled and percentaged) and the cumulative S-curve rising through it to saturation. Beneath, list the five perceived attributes as levers. Teach the relationship between the curves \u2014 the S-curve is the bell curve's integral \u2014 and have students mark where a chosen product currently sits on both.",
    "examples": "1. UPI adoption. Compatibility (works on any phone, any bank), trialability (\u20b910 transfer), observability (merchant QR everywhere) \u2014 all five attributes engineered favourably; India's fastest mass diffusion.\n2. Hybrid corn study (the origin). Rogers' foundational case: Iowa farmers adopted over 13 years, neighbours' visible yields (observability) driving the S-curve's steep middle.\n3. WhatsApp in India. Near-zero complexity and total compatibility with existing contacts produced one of the steepest consumer S-curves recorded.\n4. Solar rooftops. Strong relative advantage but weak trialability and high upfront complexity \u2014 explaining slow diffusion despite clear economics; leasing models attack exactly those attributes.\n5. ChatGPT (2022\u201323). Free trialability plus extreme observability (shared outputs) compressed the S-curve's slow phase to weeks \u2014 a modern natural experiment in the five attributes.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Forecasting and pacing the adoption of a new product or technology\n\u2022 Sequencing a launch by adopter segment \u2014 whom to win first\n\u2022 Redesigning a slow-adopting offer against the five adoption attributes\n\u2022 Planning a social or public-sector rollout (health, sanitation, digital ID)\n\u2022 Explaining to leadership why mass advertising is not moving the mainstream 49",
    "when_not": "\u2022 When treating the percentages as laws \u2014 the category shares are descriptive statistics, not planning quotas\n\u2022 For labelling people as fixed 'adopter types' \u2014 the same person is an early adopter in one category and a laggard in another\n\u2022 In coercive or mandated adoption settings (regulation, workplace tools) where social diffusion dynamics are overridden\n\u2022 As a pure marketing lens when the diffusion barrier is product design \u2014 fix compatibility or trialability before spending on persuasion",
    "sources": "\u2022 Rogers, E. M. (1962). Diffusion of Innovations. Free Press (5th ed., 2003).\n\u2022 Bass, F. M. (1969). 'A New Product Growth Model for Consumer Durables.' Management Science, 15(5), 215\u2013227 \u2014 the quantitative diffusion model."
  },
  {
    "id": 24,
    "slug": "fw-24",
    "title": "Business Model Canvas",
    "subtitle": "The whole business model on one page",
    "part": "Part III \u2014 Go-to-Market Strategy",
    "desc": "Osterwalder and Pigneur's canvas describes any business model in nine linked blocks. The right side faces the market: Customer Segments (whom we serve), Value Propositions (what problem we solve and why us), Channels (how value reaches customers), Customer Relationships (how we acquire, retain, grow), and Revenue Streams (how value is monetised). The left side faces operations: Key Resources (assets required), Key Activities (what we must do well), Key Partnerships (who helps us), and Cost Structure (what it all costs). Value proposition sits at the centre, hinging the two sides. The canvas's power is synoptic: it exposes incoherence between blocks (premium proposition, discount channels), enables rapid comparison of model alternatives, and turns 'business model' from abstraction into a designable, testable artefact.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is the standard nine-block layout: Key Partnerships, Key Activities, Key Resources on the left; Value Propositions centre; Customer Relationships, Channels, Customer Segments on the right; Cost Structure and Revenue Streams as the two base blocks. Teach the geography: right side = value creation for the market, left side = value delivery machinery, bottom = the economic equation. Have students draw arrows between blocks that must cohere \u2014 the arrows expose the model's logic and its fractures.",
    "examples": "1. Netflix vs multiplex. Same 'entertainment' proposition, radically different canvases: subscription revenue vs per-ticket; streaming channel vs physical; content library vs real estate as key resource \u2014 the canvas makes model competition visible.\n2. Gillette. Razor-and-blades drawn on the canvas: the revenue-stream block (recurring blades) reshapes every other block, from channel to cost structure.\n3. Zerodha vs full-service broker. Discount model redesigned five blocks at once: self-serve channel, technology as key resource, near-zero marginal cost \u2014 coherent low-cost architecture visible on one page.\n4. Airline low-cost vs full-service canvases. The classic side-by-side: every block differs, proving low-cost is a model, not a price.\n5. Open-source software companies. Value proposition split between free community and paying enterprise \u2014 a two- segment canvas where the free block is a key resource for the paid one.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Designing and stress-testing a new venture or business unit\n\u2022 Documenting and challenging an existing business's model\n\u2022 Comparing competing models in one industry to see why rivals monetise differently\n\u2022 Generating and screening business-model variants before building anything\n\u2022 Aligning a leadership team on how the whole model fits together on one page",
    "when_not": "\u2022 As a strategy substitute \u2014 the canvas describes a model but says nothing about competition, advantage or timing\n\u2022 When filled once and framed \u2014 its value is iteration and hypothesis testing, not documentation\n\u2022 For detailed financial planning \u2014 cost and revenue blocks are sketches, not a model you can bank\n\u2022 In stable, well-understood businesses where the nine blocks restate the obvious \u2014 use the time on the contested blocks only",
    "sources": "\u2022 Osterwalder, A. & Pigneur, Y. (2010). Business Model Generation. John Wiley & Sons.\n\u2022 Osterwalder, A. (2004). The Business Model Ontology (doctoral dissertation). University of Lausanne \u2014 the academic foundation of the canvas."
  },
  {
    "id": 25,
    "slug": "fw-25",
    "title": "Lean Startup: Build\u2013Measure\u2013Learn",
    "subtitle": "Iterative validation under extreme uncertainty",
    "part": "Part III \u2014 Go-to-Market Strategy",
    "desc": "Eric Ries's Lean Startup treats a new venture as a series of experiments rather than a plan to execute. The engine is the Build\u2013Measure\u2013Learn loop: convert the riskiest assumption into a hypothesis, build a minimum viable product (MVP) \u2014 the smallest artefact that can test it \u2014 measure real customer behaviour against innovation accounting metrics (cohorts, activation, retention \u2014 not vanity metrics like cumulative signups), and learn: persevere with the strategy, or pivot \u2014 a structured change in one element (customer segment, problem, channel, revenue model) while keeping the vision. Speed through the loop is the core competitive advantage under uncertainty: the team that learns fastest per rupee of burn wins. The method operationalises 'validated learning' as the startup's True unit of progress.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is the circular loop: Build (top) \u2192 Measure \u2192 Learn \u2192 back to Build, with Ideas entering at Build, Product as the artefact between Build and Measure, Data between Measure and Learn, and the Persevere/Pivot decision at Learn. Draw the loop with a clock in the centre \u2014 cycle time is the strategic variable \u2014 and teach the reverse- planning rule by walking the loop backwards from Learn.",
    "examples": "1. Dropbox's MVP. A three-minute demo video tested demand before the product existed \u2014 the waitlist jumped from 5,000 to 75,000 overnight, validating the riskiest assumption (do people want this?) for almost nothing.\n2. Zappos. Founder photographed shoes in local stores and bought them retail after each online order \u2014 a concierge MVP proving people would buy shoes online before building any inventory or logistics.\n3. Buffer. A pricing-page smoke test \u2014 plans and a signup button before any product \u2014 validated willingness to pay in days.\n4. Instagram's pivot. Burbn's data showed one feature (photos with filters) retained users; the zoom-in pivot discarded the rest \u2014 measurement-driven strategy change.\n5. Corporate example: a bank's video-KYC pilot. Testing one branch's customers with a manual back-office (Wizard of Oz) before building automation \u2014 lean method inside a regulated giant.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Running an early-stage venture's search for product\u2013market fit\n\u2022 Testing a new offering cheaply inside a corporate innovation team\n\u2022 Validating a risky feature, channel or pricing assumption before full build\n\u2022 Staging investment in a new product by validated learning rather than by plan\n\u2022 Deciding whether to persevere with or pivot a struggling initiative",
    "when_not": "53\n\u2022 Where failure is unacceptable or iteration is priced in lives or licences \u2014 medical devices, aviation, nuclear, core banking\n\u2022 In deep-tech ventures where years of engineering precede any testable customer artefact\n\u2022 For enterprise sales with year-long cycles \u2014 'validated learning' per loop arrives too slowly to steer weekly decisions\n\u2022 When MVPs would burn a premium brand's trust \u2014 established firms must test under flags that can afford the roughness\n\u2022 As a licence to skip strategy \u2014 a thousand experiments cannot substitute for a view on where value will pool",
    "sources": "\u2022 Ries, E. (2011). The Lean Startup. Crown Business.\n\u2022 Blank, S. (2013). 'Why the Lean Start-Up Changes Everything.' Harvard Business Review, 91(5), 63\u201372 \u2014 the customer- development basis."
  },
  {
    "id": 26,
    "slug": "fw-26",
    "title": "Blue Ocean Strategy",
    "subtitle": "Value Innovation and Reconstructing Market Boundaries",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "Developed by W. Chan Kim and Ren\u00e9e Mauborgne (2004), Blue Ocean Strategy describes the creation of new, uncontested market space (a 'Blue Ocean'), making the competition irrelevant. This contrasts with Red Ocean Strategy, where companies compete aggressively within existing industry boundaries for a share of crowded, zero-sum demand. The core engine is Value Innovation: the simultaneous pursuit of differentiation and low cost, breaking the traditional trade-off. Companies achieve this by using the ERRC Grid (Eliminate-Reduce-Raise-Create) to alter the industry's standard Strategy Canvas.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic is a Strategy Canvas plotting Competitive Factors on the horizontal axis and the level of offering/investment on the vertical axis. The standard Red Ocean curve shows industry rivals clustered together. The Blue Ocean curve diverges sharply: it drops to zero on factors that are Eliminated, dips low on factors Reduced, spikes upward on factors Raised, and introduces brand-new points on factors Created, creating a unique value curve.",
    "examples": "1. Cirque du Soleil: Entered a declining, low-margin circus industry. It eliminated animal shows and star performers, reduced three-ring danger, raised unique tent venues, and created high-art theatrical themes.\n2. Yellow Tail Wine: Entered the crowded US wine market. It eliminated complex terminology, reduced grape complexity, raised ease of selection, and created approachability.\n3. Southwest Airlines: Reconstructed regional transit. It eliminated hub-and-spoke connections, reduced turnaround times at gates, raised departure frequencies, and created point-to-point transit.\n4. Nintendo Wii: Disrupted the high-performance console wars. Nintendo eliminated performance specs, reduced graphics, raised physical accessibility, and created motion-sensing controls.\n5. Uber: Created an on-demand ride-sharing space. It eliminated street-hailing uncertainty, reduced wait times, raised pricing transparency, and created a peer-to-peer driver network.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Entering highly commoditized, low-margin industries experiencing price wars\n\u2022 Evaluating startup pitches to verify if they offer genuine value innovation\n\u2022 Structuring corporate innovation brainstorms to escape incremental feature matches",
    "when_not": "\u2022 In early-stage technology categories where baseline must-have utility is still undefined\n\u2022 When capital constraints prevent building distribution scale to protect first-mover advantages\n\u2022 In pure commodity markets where government regulation dictates rigid pricing",
    "sources": "\u2022 Kim, W. C. & Mauborgne, R. (2004). 'Blue Ocean Strategy.' Harvard Business Review, 82(10), 76\u201384.\n\u2022 Kim, W. C. & Mauborgne, R. (2005). 'Blue Ocean Strategy: How to Create Uncontested Market Space.' Harvard Business School Press."
  },
  {
    "id": 27,
    "slug": "fw-27",
    "title": "Related vs. Unrelated Diversification",
    "subtitle": "Strategic Scope, Conglomerate Discounts, and Market Contexts",
    "part": "Part I \u2014 Business Strategy",
    "desc": "This framework analyzes the performance implications of Related Diversification (sharing value chain links or channels) vs. Unrelated Diversification (expanding with no operational synergies, operating as a conglomerate). In developed markets, unrelated diversification leads to a Conglomerate Discount due to efficient capital markets and specialist firms. In emerging markets, Institutional Voids (weak capital markets, logistics, or legal frameworks) allow diversified conglomerates to earn a Conglomerate Premium by acting as efficient internal intermediaries for capital, talent, and brand trust.",
    "schematic": "How to read it. The schematic acts as a contextual filter. When passing a diversification decision through the institutional voids filter, the outcome changes: in developed markets, unrelated diversification leads to a Conglomerate Discount; in emerging markets, institutional voids enable conglomerates to capture a Conglomerate Premium by acting as internal intermediaries.",
    "examples": "1. Tata Group: Operates across steel, autos, software (TCS), hotels, tea, and retail. It leverages its trusted corporate brand and internal TAS talent pool to bypass emerging market voids in India.\n2. General Electric: A classic developed-market conglomerate that experienced high valuation discounts, leading to its eventual break-up into three separate public companies.\n3. Samsung Group: Achieved global scale by diversifying across electronics, shipbuilding, chemicals, and financial services, leveraging state support and internal capital markets.\n4. Wesfarmers: A diversified Australian conglomerate that manages retail, chemicals, and industrial products using disciplined financial metrics to outperform conglomerate assumptions.\n5. Keppel Corporation: Shifted from unrelated offshore engineering and property operations toward integrated asset management and infrastructure.",
    "when_use": "\u2022 Advising conglomerate boards on corporate restructuring, demergers, or spin-offs\n\u2022 Evaluating market entry strategy for multinational firms partnering with local diversified groups\n\u2022 Sizing investment risks in emerging economies with weak external regulatory and capital environments",
    "when_not": "\u2022 For assessing startup business plans (startups lack the scale to build internal markets)\n\u2022 For short-term financial trading or options valuation\n\u2022 In markets that have transitioned from emerging to developed where voids have closed",
    "sources": "\u2022 Khanna, T. & Palepu, K. (1997). 'Why Focused Strategies May Be Wrong for Emerging Markets.' Harvard Business Review, 75(4), 41\u201351.\n\u2022 Khanna, T. & Palepu, K. (2010). 'Winning in Emerging Markets.' Harvard Business Press.\n\u2022 Goold, M., Campbell, A. & Alexander, M. (1994). 'Corporate-Level Strategy.' John Wiley & Sons."
  }
];
